(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-cms-e6-l37-38-study-focus-set-1"]={"title":"Lectures 37–38 Study Focus — Set 1 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Four adults have community-acquired pneumonia, are stable for outpatient treatment and have no risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas. Which one fits amoxicillin as a single drug?","choices":["The one with chronic liver disease","The one with diabetes mellitus","The one with no chronic conditions","The one with asplenia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Amoxicillin, or doxycycline, is the outpatient choice when there are no comorbidities and no methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas risk. Each of the others has a listed comorbidity, which calls for a beta-lactam plus azithromycin or doxycycline, or a respiratory fluoroquinolone. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has had fever, myalgias and cough for 4 days during a week of high influenza activity. She is not hypoxemic and will be treated at home. A rapid influenza antigen test is negative. Which is the most appropriate plan?","choices":["Await a molecular test result first","No antiviral; influenza is excluded","No antiviral after 48 hours","Start oseltamivir now"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Age 65 or older and chronic pulmonary disease make her high-risk, and risk status supports prompt antiviral treatment even after 48 hours. A negative rapid antigen test does not exclude influenza during high activity, and indicated antivirals are not withheld while awaiting confirmation. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 95, 97, 105, 108"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man who aspirated gastric contents during a witnessed seizure was managed with oxygen and airway support and no antibiotic. Three days later he is worse, with a new fever, purulent sputum and a persistent infiltrate. Which complication should be suspected?","choices":["Resolving chemical injury","Asthma exacerbation","Viral acute bronchitis","Secondary bacterial infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pneumonitis is managed with support, and persistence or worsening raises concern for secondary infection, a complication or another diagnosis. New fever, purulent sputum and a persistent infiltrate after 3 days point to a secondary bacterial infection, which now needs the applicable pneumonia regimen. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the main limitation of a positive Pneumocystis polymerase chain reaction result?","choices":["It has low sensitivity","It can detect colonization","It cannot be run on induced sputum","It needs a lung biopsy specimen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Polymerase chain reaction for Pneumocystis on induced sputum or bronchoalveolar lavage is highly sensitive but can detect colonization, so a positive result is interpreted with the symptoms, the imaging and the organism burden rather than read as proof of pneumonia. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 65, 67, 69"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman with human immunodeficiency virus infection starts trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for Pneumocystis pneumonia. Her arterial oxygen pressure on room air is 61 mm Hg. Which therapy should be added?","choices":["An inhaled corticosteroid","A systemic corticosteroid","Intravenous pentamidine","No adjunct is needed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adjunctive corticosteroids are indicated in Pneumocystis pneumonia linked to human immunodeficiency virus when the room-air arterial oxygen pressure is below 70 mm Hg, and hers is 61. Prednisone is started as early as possible, ideally within 72 hours of starting treatment. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman who works in a setting with ongoing tuberculosis transmission has a positive interferon-gamma release assay on targeted testing. She has no symptoms, her examination is normal and her chest radiograph is normal. Which best describes her tuberculosis status?","choices":["Pulmonary tuberculosis disease","Progressive primary tuberculosis","Reactivation (secondary) tuberculosis","Latent tuberculosis infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A positive interferon-gamma release assay shows infection, and her clinical evaluation found no evidence of disease: no symptoms, a normal examination and a normal radiograph, which is usual in latent infection. Latent infection is asymptomatic and not infectious. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 10, 19, 29"},{"q":"Alongside clinical judgment, which tool is preferred to support the admission decision in community-acquired pneumonia?","choices":["White blood cell count","Pneumonia Severity Index","Chest radiograph extent","Sputum culture result"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The Pneumonia Severity Index plus clinical judgment is preferred for admission decisions; the five-item confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure and age score complements it but does not replace clinical judgment. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 29, 31"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with bronchiectasis has had a chronic cough with sputum for months. Her sputum acid-fast bacilli smear is positive. What does this smear result establish?","choices":["Pulmonary tuberculosis disease","Nontuberculous mycobacterial lung disease","Latent tuberculosis infection","Acid-fast organisms, species not yet known"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An acid-fast smear identifies a group of organisms, the acid-fast bacilli with lipid-rich, mycolic acid cell walls. It does not distinguish Mycobacterium tuberculosis from nontuberculous mycobacteria, and bronchiectasis raises the risk of nontuberculous disease, so the species still has to be identified. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 4, 5"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman who is 14 weeks pregnant comes in during October and asks about vaccines. Which vaccine should be avoided during her pregnancy?","choices":["Recombinant influenza vaccine","Live attenuated influenza vaccine","Inactivated influenza vaccine","Tdap during this pregnancy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In pregnancy, influenza vaccination uses an age-appropriate inactivated or recombinant vaccine in any trimester, and the live attenuated vaccine is avoided. Live attenuated vaccine has pregnancy and immunocompromise restrictions, so her pregnancy rules it out. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 5, 6, 124"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man who received BCG (bacille Calmette-Guérin) vaccine in infancy needs testing for tuberculosis infection before starting work in a setting with ongoing transmission. He has no symptoms. Which test is preferred?","choices":["Chest computed tomography","Tuberculin skin test","Interferon-gamma release assay","Acid-fast sputum smear"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bacille Calmette-Guérin vaccination can cause a false-positive skin test, and the blood test is preferred for most vaccinated patients because the vaccine does not cause a positive interferon-gamma release assay. It measures immune responses to tuberculosis-specific antigens and needs one visit. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 21, 24"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with untreated human immunodeficiency virus infection cleaned out a barn full of bird droppings 3 weeks ago. He is admitted to the intensive care unit with high fever, hypoxemia, hypotension and diffuse infiltrates, and his urine Histoplasma antigen is positive. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Oral itraconazole as an outpatient","Fluconazole for 6 to 12 months","Observation without an antifungal","Amphotericin-based induction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Histoplasmosis treatment follows severity and host risk. An intensive care admission with hypoxemia, hypotension and diffuse infiltrates in an immunocompromised host is severe disease, and severe pulmonary or disseminated histoplasmosis requires specialist-led amphotericin-based induction. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 75, 77, 78, 123"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man has had a cough for 6 days with yellow sputum. His temperature is 101.8°F and his respiratory rate 24/min, and there are crackles with dullness to percussion over the left lower lobe. He asks for an antibiotic because his sputum is yellow. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["An antibiotic for the yellow sputum","A sputum culture before deciding","A chest radiograph to confirm pneumonia","Supportive care for bronchitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fever, a raised respiratory rate and focal crackles with dullness are the findings that raise concern for pneumonia rather than uncomplicated bronchitis, and suspected pneumonia is confirmed with a chest radiograph. The yellow sputum is not what changes the plan: the fever and focal findings are. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 15, 16, 18, 29"},{"q":"After a recent tuberculosis exposure, a first tuberculin skin test is negative. When is the test repeated?","choices":["2-4 weeks after the last exposure","4-6 weeks after the last exposure","8-10 weeks after the last exposure","16-20 weeks after the last exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Early infection can produce a negative skin test or blood assay, so testing is repeated 8 to 10 weeks after the last exposure when indicated. Symptoms or high clinical suspicion still require disease evaluation now. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which group uses a tuberculin skin test induration cut-off of 5 mm or more?","choices":["People with no risk factors","Residents of congregate settings","Children under 5 years","Recent close contacts"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The 5 mm cut-off applies to human immunodeficiency virus infection, recent close contacts, transplant recipients, major immunosuppression, and imaging suggesting prior tuberculosis. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with community-acquired pneumonia is intubated for respiratory failure and admitted to the intensive care unit. He has no validated risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas. Which empiric regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Levofloxacin monotherapy","Vancomycin plus cefepime","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Ceftriaxone as a single drug"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mechanical ventilation makes this severe community-acquired pneumonia, treated with a beta-lactam plus azithromycin or a respiratory fluoroquinolone. Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas coverage is added only for validated risks, which he lacks; cultures are obtained and therapy de-escalated later. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 44, 45"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with ventilator-associated pneumonia has had an adequate response to culture-directed antibiotics. What is the usual total duration of therapy?","choices":["3 days","5 days","7 days","14 days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia, a 7-day course is usual when the response is adequate, adjusted to clinical response, with prompt de-escalation as cultures clarify the diagnosis. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 46, 49"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has been intubated in the intensive care unit for 4 days after a stroke. He develops a new fever, purulent endotracheal secretions and a new infiltrate, and his blood pressure is falling. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Chest computed tomography first","Hold antibiotics until cultures return","Cultures, then empiric antibiotics","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A new infiltrate with fever and purulent secretions more than 48 hours after intubation is ventilator-associated pneumonia. Respiratory specimens are collected, then appropriate empiric antibiotics start without delay, because he is becoming unstable; therapy is later narrowed to the culture results. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 25, 37, 42, 49"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with no medical conditions has never received a pneumococcal vaccine. Which pneumococcal vaccination is appropriate for him?","choices":["Yearly pneumococcal vaccination","One 20-valent conjugate vaccine dose","Wait until he turns 65","A 15-valent conjugate vaccine alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All adults aged 50 or older who have never had a pneumococcal conjugate vaccine, or whose history is unknown, are vaccinated. One dose of the 20-valent or 21-valent conjugate vaccine completes the series; a 15-valent conjugate vaccine is usually followed by the 23-valent polysaccharide vaccine. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 8, 9, 124"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a single sputum culture that grows Mycobacterium avium complex at a routine visit. His symptoms are at baseline and no chest imaging has been done. Which best describes his status?","choices":["Pulmonary tuberculosis","Diagnostic criteria not met","Nontuberculous lung disease","Latent tuberculosis infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A respiratory isolate alone does not prove nontuberculous disease. Diagnosis requires all domains: compatible symptoms and imaging, exclusion of alternatives and usually 2 or more sputum cultures with the same species. He has one culture, no new symptoms and no imaging. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 38, 39"},{"q":"A 43-year-old woman with suspected pulmonary tuberculosis has three sputum specimens collected. Which testing should be requested on the specimens?","choices":["Acid-fast smear, culture and a molecular test","Gram stain and routine bacterial culture","Acid-fast smear, then stop if negative","Molecular test in place of culture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The specimens go for acid-fast smear and mycobacterial culture, a rapid molecular test is performed on at least one respiratory specimen, and molecular and culture-based drug-susceptibility testing is obtained. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman meets the criteria for macrolide-susceptible Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease, without cavitation or severe disease. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Azithromycin alone until cultures clear","Inhaled liposomal amikacin as first therapy","Isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, ethambutol","Azithromycin, ethambutol and a rifamycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Macrolide-susceptible Mycobacterium avium complex pulmonary disease is treated with azithromycin, ethambutol and a rifamycin, under specialist direction, continued for at least 12 months after culture conversion. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man is admitted with coronavirus disease 2019. His oxygen saturation is 90% on room air, and he needs supplemental oxygen. Which medication is the core treatment?","choices":["Dexamethasone","Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir","Azithromycin","Molnupiravir"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hospitalized adults with hypoxemia receive dexamethasone, usually for up to 10 days or until discharge, with remdesivir considered by timing and respiratory support. Oxygenation and severity, not simply a positive test or elapsed time, decide the pathway, and his saturation of 90% puts him on it. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 121, 122, 126"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has a positive interferon-gamma release assay on targeted testing. He has no symptoms. Which next step is most appropriate?","choices":["Repeat the assay in 8 to 10 weeks","Clinical evaluation and chest imaging","Airborne isolation and health reporting","Start latent-infection treatment today"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A positive interferon-gamma release assay indicates infection, but neither it nor the skin test can distinguish latent infection from disease. A positive result requires clinical evaluation and chest imaging, so disease is excluded before any latent-infection treatment. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 19, 24"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with dysphagia after a stroke is admitted to a general ward with nonsevere aspiration pneumonia and started on ceftriaxone plus azithromycin. He has never had methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus or Pseudomonas isolated. On day 2, imaging newly shows a pleural empyema. Which change to his antibiotics is appropriate?","choices":["Add anaerobic coverage","Add vancomycin","No change to the regimen","Stop antibiotics; this is pneumonitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aspiration pneumonia is treated with the community or hospital regimen for its setting and severity, and anaerobic coverage is added only when abscess or empyema is suspected, not routinely. The new empyema is that trigger; drainage is also considered when indicated. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 38, 40, 50, 51, 126"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man admitted with nonsevere community-acquired pneumonia is improving on ceftriaxone plus azithromycin. On day 3 his blood cultures grow Streptococcus pneumoniae that is susceptible to narrower agents. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Switch to cefepime","Narrow to the culture results","Continue both for 14 days","Add vancomycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics are reassessed daily and narrowed when possible, and therapy is narrowed to culture and susceptibility results. He is improving and his organism and its susceptibilities are known, so broad empiric coverage is no longer needed; duration follows clinical stability. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 38, 40, 45"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man's chest radiograph and sputum smear lead his clinician to presume pulmonary tuberculosis; culture results are weeks away. Which reporting step is appropriate now?","choices":["Notify the tuberculosis program now","Report after culture confirmation","Report if drug resistance is found","No report; the clinician manages it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The local or state tuberculosis program is notified of presumed or confirmed tuberculosis, so reporting does not wait for culture. Public-health coordination also guides the criteria for ending isolation. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman received a respiratory syncytial virus vaccine last year and asks whether she needs another dose this fall. Which counseling statement is accurate?","choices":["A second dose this fall","Revaccinate every year","Revaccinate every 10 years","No repeat dose is recommended"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Respiratory syncytial virus vaccination is one dose for all adults aged 75 or older and for adults 50 to 74 at increased risk, and repeat annual dosing is not currently recommended. She already had her one dose. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 7, 124"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman develops ventilator-associated pneumonia on day 5 of mechanical ventilation. Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is common in her unit. Which empiric regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Cefepime plus vancomycin","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Cefepime as a single drug","Moxifloxacin plus linezolid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Local methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus prevalence adds vancomycin or linezolid to an antipseudomonal beta-lactam such as cefepime. Cultures are obtained, and therapy is reassessed daily and de-escalated. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 46, 42, 48"},{"q":"For suspected Pneumocystis pneumonia with a negative induced sputum and persistent high suspicion, which test comes next?","choices":["Bronchoalveolar lavage","Pulmonary function testing","Expectorated sputum","Lung biopsy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A negative induced specimen does not exclude Pneumocystis pneumonia; bronchoalveolar lavage is considered when induced sputum is negative or unavailable and suspicion remains high, sending microscopy or polymerase chain reaction testing. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 67, 69, 68"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman who keeps several parakeets at home is diagnosed with psittacosis and started on doxycycline. One of the birds has been sick. Which additional step is appropriate?","choices":["Veterinary assessment of the birds","Respiratory isolation at home","Reporting is not required","Prophylaxis for household contacts"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management includes arranging veterinary assessment of implicated birds and safe cleaning of contaminated areas, reporting according to state requirements and involving public health for outbreaks. Her sick parakeet is the likely source and can infect others. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 87"}]};
